-- title:
--   Recursive inverse Fermat-ring FFT (decimation in time) is correct within its cost
-- statement:
--   Let `pT` be a procedure index whose body is `ditBody pT` and let $n\ge 1$; write $F=2^n+1$. For every $j$, every state $s$, every list $B$ of integers and every $E\in\mathbb N$, assume
--   - $|B| = 2^j$, and either $j=0$ or $2^j E = 2n$ (so $2^E$ is a $2^j$-th root of unity mod $F$, as $2^{2n}\equiv 1$);
--   - every entry of $B$ is a canonical residue, $0\le b<F$;
--   - stack `DX` holds the items of $B$ (first entry on top) followed by $R$; `DL` has $2^j$ on top, `DE` has $2^E$ on top, `RN` has $2^n$ and `RF` has $F$ on top.
--
--   Then calling `pT` runs from $s$ to the state in which `DX` holds $\mathrm{ditZ}(F,n,E,j,B)$ followed by $R$, all other stacks unchanged:
--   $$\mathrm{IRuns}\ I\ \mathrm{iprog}\ (\mathrm{call}\ pT)\ s\ \big(s[\mathrm{DX} := \mathrm{ints}(\mathrm{ditZ}(F,n,E,j,B))\mathbin{+\!\!+}R]\big)\ \big(500000\,(n+3)(j+1)\,2^j\big).$$
--   Here `ditZ` is the reference recursive decimation-in-time transform: it transforms the two halves recursively with $2E$ and combines them with butterflies whose twiddle factors are powers of $2$, realised as shifts followed by reduction mod $F$.
--
--   This is the inverse transform of the Schönhage–Strassen algorithm; with $j=k$ and $E=2Q$ it is applied once per recursion level, at cost $O(n\,k\,2^k)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** States are `IState NS` records with item stacks `G : Fin NS → List Item` (head = top), input pointer `ip` and output `out`; `s.upd X L` replaces stack `X` by `L`; `ints A` is the list of integer items of `A`. The named stacks (`ARG`, `PRM`, `RN`, `RF`, `DX`, `DL`, …) are fixed indices in `Fin 32`. `IRuns I iprog p s s' c` means that `p` executes from `s` to exactly `s'` with some cost $c' \le c$, so costs are upper bounds; the program text `iprog` and input `I` are arbitrary unless constrained.
-- source:
--   A. Schönhage, V. Strassen, Schnelle Multiplikation großer Zahlen, Computing 7 (1971), 281–292 (main theorem: O(n log n log log n) multitape TM multiplication). Lemma `NLogN.dit_runs` of the Lean development NLogN/SSDit.lean (lines 317–539), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

-- Schönhage–Strassen multitape-TM development (NLogN), generated by skeleton subtraction.

import Definitions.Def_IntMul_SS_Defs2

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false
set_option maxHeartbeats 1000000

open NLogN
open IntMul
variable {P : ℕ} {I : List Sym} {iprog : Fin P → IStmt NS P}

theorem NLogN.dit_runs (pT : Fin P) (hP : iprog pT = ditBody pT) (n : ℕ) (hn : 1 ≤ n) :
    ∀ j (s : IState NS) (B : List ℤ) (R LL LE LN LF : List Item) (E : ℕ),
      B.length = 2 ^ j → (j = 0 ∨ 2 ^ j * E = 2 * n) → Canon (2 ^ n + 1) B →
      s.G DX = ints B ++ R → s.G DL = .int (2 ^ j) :: LL → s.G DE = .int (2 ^ E) :: LE →
      s.G RN = .int (2 ^ n) :: LN → s.G RF = .int (2 ^ n + 1) :: LF →
      IRuns I iprog (.call pT) s (s.upd DX (ints (ditZ (2 ^ n + 1) n E j B) ++ R))
        (ditCost n j) := by sorry
